Register components declared in split manifests, not just base.apk

An app built with android:isolatedSplits="true" declares its components in a split
APK's manifest rather than in the base's. Chrome is one: base.apk declares three
Play Core / GMS helper activities and no launcher at all, while the launcher
activity com.google.android.apps.chrome.Main lives in split_chrome.apk together
with 54 others.

Bcore parsed only base.apk, so such a package reached the component resolver with
no launchable activity. queryIntentActivities(MAIN/LAUNCHER) then returned empty,
getLaunchIntentForPackage() returned null, launchApk() returned false, and the app
could never be started.

Patches 0003 and 0004 already give a split install its runtime paths -- the guest's
ClassLoader sees splitSourceDirs and native libraries are extracted from the ABI
split -- but neither reaches manifest parsing, which happens earlier and separately.

The fix hands the framework parser the install's directory instead of its base APK.
The parser then takes its own cluster path, parsing the base plus every split and
merging the components into one Package. That is the same code the platform runs to
install the app, so component identity, de-duplication and isolated-split resource
namespacing are handled there rather than re-implemented here, and BPackage converts
the merged result exactly as it already converted a monolithic one.

The directory is only ever derived from what the host PackageManager reports for the
package being installed, and the APK we were asked to install must be that same base
APK path, with every split required to sit in that one directory. A guest-supplied or
imported path therefore never qualifies, so this cannot be used to register unrelated
components under a package. Anything unexpected falls back to the previous behaviour
of parsing the single file.

No change to launchApk, getLaunchIntentForPackage, queryIntentActivities, UID
handling, package identity, signatures or certificates.

